WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.800
Hi, everyone. Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 114. It's July 26th,

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:13.340
2026. We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today. Let's dive in. Here's

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:18.140
something that's going to change how you think about creating games from scratch. A researcher

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:24.120
shared an experiment where an AI system built an entire working city builder game called Cezanne.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.480
The game turns simple brushstrokes on a canvas into a living town that grows on its own.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:36.480
You paint with gestures and the AI turns those marks into streets, houses, and neighborhoods

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.420
that develop their own personalities over time. The whole concept came from the AI itself,

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:48.480
blending an impressionist art style with city planning rules. Think of it like dropping seeds on

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.540
soil and watching a garden decide its own layout instead of following a fixed plan.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.520
Another way to picture it is finger painting on a rainy window,

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:02.680
except the smears turn into buildings that keep evolving long after you lift your finger.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.860
The AI did not copy an existing game from somewhere. It invented the idea of mixing art gestures

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:16.740
with urban growth so every stroke shapes long-term patterns. This matters because it shows AI helping

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:22.860
people invent new creative tools instead of just answering questions. For anyone who enjoys games

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:28.820
like Minecraft or The Sims, this points toward more personal ways to build worlds where your own

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:35.000
drawing set the rules. Students interested in game design or digital art could sketch an idea and see

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:41.440
a working prototype appear quickly. The demo already runs free in any web browser with nothing to

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:47.680
install. Go to the Cezanne site in your browser, paint a few shapes, and notice how a curved stroke

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:53.440
might create a different neighborhood feel than a straight one. That single idea shows AI moving

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.580
from chat windows into hands on creative experiments anyone can play with right now.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:04.660
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive where we unpack how something actually

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:10.780
works. Today we are looking at how language models turn simple predictions into brand new ideas

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:16.400
like the Cezanne game. The process starts with something you already know from your phone. Your

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:21.260
keyboard guesses the next word while you text by looking at everything you have typed before

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:27.880
and what usually follows in similar messages. The model does the same thing but on a much larger scale.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:34.500
It looks across millions of examples of games, art styles, and city building mechanics all at once.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:40.880
Next, it predicts which fresh combination would feel interesting and fun instead of just finishing

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.960
a sentence. Then it fills in the details step by step, such as neighborhoods that change character

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:53.860
or gestures that grow into buildings. The model is not copying one single game. It blends patterns it

00:02:53.860 --> 00:03:00.180
has seen across thousands of creative projects to invent something new on the spot. Picture a recipe box

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:06.160
where every card holds one cooking trick you have tried before. The model flips through the entire

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:12.420
box and combines three or four tricks in a way no one has written down yet. That chaining of learned

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:18.520
patterns is exactly how the AI came up with an impressionist city builder from scratch. The result

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:24.320
feels like invention because the system has seen so many human creations that it can remix them into

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:30.560
something that did not exist before. Once you see that prediction can lead to invention, the whole process

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:36.340
stops feeling mysterious and starts feeling like a very fast, very well read brainstorming partner.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:41.820
Let's move into cool stuff and try this, where I share tools you can open right now.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:48.400
First up is a free beginner guide that explains what AI agents are and how regular people can use them.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:54.700
AI agents act like helpful assistants that can take multiple steps on their own instead of waiting

00:03:54.700 --> 00:04:00.840
for one question at a time. This becomes useful if you have ever wished a chatbot could keep working

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:08.760
on a project while you step away. Visit news.google.com and search for the title, how to use AI agents in

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:15.700
2026, a complete beginner friendly guide. Read the first section and try the simple example it gives

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:22.540
using any free chatbot you already have on your phone or laptop. Next, one person discovered their

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:28.860
new MacBook had malware after asking a chatbot to help lower memory use for running local AI models.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:35.180
The chatbot spotted suspicious files pretending to be normal services and helped trace the problem back

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:41.540
to earlier software installs. This shows how AI can serve as a second pair of eyes when troubleshooting

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:48.960
everyday tech issues. If your own laptop feels slower than expected lately, open any free chatbot and

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:54.940
describe what you are seeing, such as higher memory use right after startup. Then follow the questions

00:04:54.940 --> 00:05:01.700
it asks step by step to check startup items. Both of these experiments require zero coding and work

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:08.420
entirely in a browser or an app you already use. Time for quick bits, short stories that caught my

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:14.940
eye this week. A Canadian politician read an entire AI chatbot prompt out loud during a parliament

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:21.240
session. The politician spoke the full prompt directly into the official record while the session was

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:27.400
underway. This event occurred in a Canadian parliamentary setting and was reported through aggregated

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:33.860
news sources. The action illustrated how chatbot prompts are moving beyond private use into formal

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:39.900
government proceedings. Observers noted that the moment reflected the speed at which these tools are

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:46.880
appearing in non-technical public spaces. The incident also underscored that AI prompts are no longer

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:53.460
confined to technology discussions alone. Coverage of the event appeared in a Google news feed linking

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:59.700
to parliamentary records. In another story, someone shared that they chat with an AI named Dolly

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:05.740
for company during lonely evenings when their spouse travels. The person described turning to Dolly

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:11.200
specifically on nights when their partner was away. They framed the conversations as personal

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:17.820
companionship rather than task-based queries. The account appeared in a Google news compilation of

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:24.080
user experiences with chatbots. The individual emphasized that the AI functioned as a supportive

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:30.860
presence during those periods. This example shows one instance of users forming ongoing conversational

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:37.020
relationships with particular AI systems. Reports of the interaction were collected under a Google

